  <article class="almanac-prose">
    <section id="wire" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.1 · authenticated transport</span>
        <h2>The request has nowhere to claim authority.</h2>
        <p><code>dekopon-run</code> opens one fresh Unix connection, validates private socket metadata and the configured server UID, writes one bounded frame, reads one bounded response, and closes the connection.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="authority-state-machine">
        <div><span>client may send</span><strong>proposal fields</strong><small>capability · JSON input · invocation ID · trace ID · traceParent</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">private Unix socket →</i>
        <div class="authority-broker-box">
          <span>kernel + owner-controlled mapping</span>
          <div><strong>peer UID</strong><b>→</b><strong>principal + actor</strong><b>→</b><strong>policy context</strong></div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→ typed result</i>
        <div><span>client may receive</span><strong>result + public evidence</strong><small>never a transferable grant</small></div>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Present on the invocation wire</th><th scope="col">Deliberately impossible on the wire</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>protocol version and strict frame shape</td><td>principal or actor claim</td></tr>
            <tr><td>unique invocation and durable trace identifiers</td><td>policy decision or revision supplied by caller</td></tr>
            <tr><td>capability ID and bounded untrusted JSON input</td><td>HTTP destinations, methods, or execution constraints</td></tr>
            <tr><td>optional well-formed W3C <code>traceParent</code></td><td>credentials or <code>AuthorizedInvocation</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Mode <code>0600</code> makes the socket one UID trust domain. Every process running as that UID can use its configured actor, so operators should give the broker/client role a dedicated UID where that distinction matters. This is a documented local boundary, not per-process attestation or multi-tenant identity.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="lifecycle" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.2 · current state machine</span>
        <h2>Nine stages keep identity, permission, work, and record aligned.</h2>
      </div>

      <ol class="mental-model-list">
        <li><span>01</span><div><strong>Authenticate the connection.</strong><p>The server derives peer UID from the connected stream and resolves one exact owner-controlled mapping. Unmapped peers learn no capability inventory.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>02</span><div><strong>Decode under hard bounds.</strong><p>A four-byte length prefix is checked before allocation; the entire read/write has one deadline; unknown JSON fields fail.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>03</span><div><strong>Reserve the invocation ID.</strong><p>Replay IDs restored from verified audit history and accepted in the current process cannot be reused—even for a previously denied proposal.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>04</span><div><strong>Resolve trusted metadata.</strong><p>The broker, not the manifest or model, selects the configured provider/capability metadata used by policy.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>05</span><div><strong>Evaluate exact deny-by-default policy.</strong><p>Principal, actor, capability, provider, effect, idempotency, and bounded constraints must all match.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>06</span><div><strong>Append the decision.</strong><p>Every decoded invocation from a mapped peer gets a decision event before provider work begins.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>07</span><div><strong>Create and consume authorization locally.</strong><p>A private, non-cloneable <code>AuthorizedInvocation</code> binds proposal, decision, receipt, provider, and constraints inside the broker process.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>08</span><div><strong>Execute in a fresh bounded Store.</strong><p>The host applies exact HTTP grants beneath independent ceilings and collects sanitized call evidence that guest code cannot widen or mask.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>09</span><div><strong>Append outcome and advance checkpoint.</strong><p>The broker links outcome, digest, evidence, decision, invocation, and trace; then atomically updates the retained count/head checkpoint.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="policy" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.3 · permissioning</span>
        <h2>Permission narrows at every layer.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="data-journey-map">
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-remote">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>untrusted</span><strong>proposal</strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node"><small>asks for</small><strong><code>jsonplaceholder.posts.create</code></strong><code>input cannot assert actor</code></div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓ exact context rule</i>
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-host">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>trusted broker code</span><strong>authorization</strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node-row">
            <div class="journey-node"><small>identity</small><strong>principal + actor</strong><code>from peer mapping</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">+</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>operation</small><strong>capability + provider</strong><code>external-write · non-idempotent</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">+</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>constraints</small><strong>exact HTTP grant</strong><code>authority · POST · 1 call</code></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓ consumed, never returned</i>
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-guest">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>bounded execution</span><strong>provider + native host</strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node-row">
            <div class="journey-node"><small>guest</small><strong>build request</strong><code>cannot set auth headers</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→ verify →</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>native engine</small><strong>pin + send</strong><code>no proxy · no redirect</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→ record →</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>evidence</small><strong>sanitized metadata</strong><code>no body or URL path</code></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>Rust typestate makes accidental fabrication and reuse harder, but it is defense in depth. The real control is the separately deployed process, authenticated socket, trusted mapping, exact policy, single-use grant bound to execution, constrained linker, and durable ordering. No one layer is advertised as a substitute for the others.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="audit" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.4 · integrity and replay</span>
        <h2>Audit is not a printf after the effect.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="run-mode-cards">
        <article class="run-mode-current">
          <div>{{ status("current", "Per record") }}<span>owner-only JSONL</span></div>
          <h3>Hash-linked append</h3>
          <p>The file is opened without following symlinks, exclusively writer-locked, bounded, verified before append, and synchronized after each decision and outcome.</p>
          <ul><li>partial records fail startup</li><li>content or link mutation fails verification</li><li>accepted invocation IDs rebuild replay state</li></ul>
        </article>
        <article class="run-mode-future">
          <div>{{ status("current", "Per append") }}<span>separate local file</span></div>
          <h3>Atomic count/head checkpoint</h3>
          <p>After audit sync, the service synchronizes and atomically replaces a separately locked checkpoint, then synchronizes its parent directory.</p>
          <ul><li>startup requires an exact verified prefix</li><li>one-record audit-ahead crash window is recoverable</li><li>larger gaps or rollback fail closed</li></ul>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p>The checkpoint makes valid-prefix truncation detectable relative to retained local state. It does not make the host tamper-proof: an attacker who can coordinate rollback or deletion of both files can defeat local comparison. Independent retention, signing, or remote anchoring is the next explicit milestone.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="failures" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.5 · retry contract</span>
        <h2>The failure code tells the operator whether work may have happened.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Code / outcome</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th><th scope="col">Retry posture</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>unauthenticated</code></th><td>Connected UID has no trusted mapping.</td><td>Do not retry until mapping changes.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>invalid-request</code></th><td>Strict bounded frame did not decode.</td><td>Correct it; use a fresh invocation ID.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>broker-unavailable</code></th><td>No provider work began.</td><td>Safe to resubmit with a fresh invocation ID.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>outcome-unaudited</code></th><td>Provider work may have completed but terminal audit failed.</td><td><strong>Do not resubmit.</strong> Reconcile manually.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Denied result</th><td>Policy decision was recorded; provider did not run.</td><td>Change policy or proposal—not the ID.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Failed result</th><td>Authorized provider attempt completed with failure and was audited.</td><td>Respect idempotency/effect semantics before any new proposal.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>The client never retries automatically. A lost response to a non-idempotent external write is an unknown outcome; inventing a new invocation ID and trying again would bypass replay rejection rather than solve the ambiguity.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="limits" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.6 · current deployment posture</span>
        <h2>A mature local foundation, not a production multi-tenant claim.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="truth-label-cards">
        <article><div>{{ status("current", "Shipped") }}</div><h3>Local Unix broker</h3><p>Secure socket lifecycle, exact peer-UID mapping, bounded concurrency and shutdown, constrained providers, evidence, audit, checkpoint reconciliation, and OTLP spans.</p></article>
        <article><div>{{ status("direction", "Next") }}</div><h3>Credential resolver</h3><p>Provider credentials remain an architectural boundary but v0.2.0 injects none. Destination-bound secret resolution needs its own redaction and authority tests.</p></article>
        <article><div>{{ status("direction", "Next") }}</div><h3>Stronger deployment</h3><p>Independent checkpoint anchoring, non-Unix authenticated transport, per-process identity, tenancy, key management, and incident automation remain future work.</p></article>
      </div>

